package com.hpcai270

// Days 203–219: containers, Kubernetes, Slurm and AI workload orchestration.
val hpcDaysPart18 = listOf(
Day(203,"Containers fundamentals","Package the application, not the server","Containers isolate application userspace while sharing the host kernel. In AI infrastructure they improve reproducibility and deployment consistency.","MODEL\n\nImage → container runtime → namespaces/cgroups → host kernel → GPU/NIC/storage devices.","STORAGE\n\nContainerized applications still need data. CSI, host mounts, NFS, parallel filesystems and object clients connect containers to storage.","LAB\n\nRun a container that reads a mounted dataset and inspect its namespace and resource limits.","INTERVIEW\n\nContainer versus VM: containers share a kernel; VMs virtualize a full guest OS. Discuss isolation and operational trade-offs.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand what a container changes and what it does not."),
Day(204,"Container images","Layers, registries and reproducibility","An image is a versioned artifact. Production AI platforms depend on trusted registries, vulnerability scanning and reproducible tags/digests.","FLOW\n\nDockerfile/Build → registry → node cache → container runtime → process.","SECURITY\n\nPrefer immutable digests, minimal images, signed/trusted artifacts and controlled registries. Never bake credentials into images.","AI\n\nCUDA libraries, Python packages and framework versions must align with host drivers and GPU runtime support.","LAB\n\nBuild a small Python image, push it to a test registry and run by digest. Record the exact dependency versions.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is ‘latest’ a poor production tag? It is mutable and can silently change the workload.","GOAL\n\nTreat images as infrastructure artifacts."),
Day(205,"GPU containers","Passing accelerators safely","GPU workloads require host driver/runtime compatibility and a mechanism such as NVIDIA Container Toolkit or Kubernetes GPU plugins to expose devices.","PATH\n\nApplication container → CUDA runtime libraries → host NVIDIA driver → GPU device.","FAILURES\n\nImage works on one node but not another due to driver mismatch, GPU capability, library incompatibility or device-plugin state.","LAB\n\nMap a containerized GPU workload's dependencies: image, CUDA, driver, GPU model and orchestration layer.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy does the CUDA version inside the image not completely determine compatibility? The host driver is also part of the runtime contract.","GOAL\n\nDebug GPU containers from host to application."),
Day(206,"Kubernetes architecture","Control plane and workers","Kubernetes manages desired state for containerized workloads. Learn API server, scheduler, controller manager, etcd and kubelet before learning YAML.","FLOW\n\nUser/API → API server → etcd state → controllers/scheduler → kubelet → container runtime → workload.","STORAGE\n\nPersistentVolume/PersistentVolumeClaim and CSI connect workloads to storage systems.","AI\n\nGPU scheduling, distributed jobs, data staging and shared storage make Kubernetes a complex infrastructure layer.","LAB\n\nDraw a pod lifecycle from YAML submission to a running process on a worker node.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat happens when a worker node dies? Explain control-plane detection, rescheduling and persistent data implications.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand Kubernetes as a distributed control system."),
Day(207,"Kubernetes scheduling","GPU placement and resource requests","The scheduler places pods based on resources, constraints, affinity and policy. AI workloads add GPU and topology requirements.","MODEL\n\nPod request → scheduler filters nodes → scores candidates → bind → kubelet starts workload.","AI\n\nGPU count, memory, node labels, topology and network/storage locality can influence placement.","LAB\n\nDesign scheduling rules for a 4-GPU pod that must use one node and a dataset mount.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is ‘the cluster has 100 free GPUs’ insufficient to prove a 4-GPU job can start? Placement, fragmentation, topology and resource reservations matter.","GOAL\n\nThink in schedulable resources, not totals."),
Day(208,"Kubernetes storage and CSI","Connecting pods to real storage","CSI provides a standardized interface for storage plugins. Learn volume lifecycle and the difference between ephemeral and persistent data.","FLOW\n\nPVC → storage class/controller → CSI driver → storage system → volume → node/pod mount.","AI\n\nShared filesystems, block devices and object clients serve different workload needs. A training pod may need shared read access; a database may need block semantics.","LAB\n\nDraw PVC-to-storage architecture for NFS and for a distributed block system. Identify controller and node responsibilities.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat happens if a pod moves to another node? Persistent storage must be attachable/mountable on the new node with correct identity and semantics.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand Kubernetes storage from API to backend."),
Day(209,"Kubernetes networking","Pod traffic, services and CNI","Kubernetes adds a logical network layer over physical infrastructure. AI clusters still depend on the underlying NICs, switches and routing.","LAYERS\n\nPod network → CNI → node network → physical fabric. Services add virtual discovery/load balancing.","AI\n\nDistributed training may need high-performance networking and topology awareness. Ordinary service networking is not automatically equivalent to HPC fabric performance.","LAB\n\nTrace a pod-to-pod packet path conceptually and identify where packet encapsulation or virtual switching can add overhead.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy might a Kubernetes network be unsuitable for a high-performance collective without additional design? Discuss bandwidth, latency, RDMA and device plugins/operators.","GOAL\n\nConnect cloud-native networking to physical AI fabrics."),
Day(210,"Slurm architecture","The HPC resource scheduler","Slurm allocates cluster resources to jobs. Learn controller, compute daemon, partitions, jobs, steps, accounts and fair-share concepts.","FLOW\n\nUser → sbatch/srun → Slurm controller → scheduling policy → compute nodes → job step → application.","STORAGE\n\nSlurm allocation can determine which nodes access shared storage concurrently. Storage capacity and fabric are not independent of scheduling.","LAB\n\nWrite sample batch jobs requesting GPUs, CPUs, memory and a time limit. Explain each directive.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat is the difference between a job and a job step? Explain resource allocation versus process execution.","GOAL\n\nBe comfortable reading and writing Slurm jobs."),
Day(211,"Slurm GPU scheduling","Allocating scarce accelerators","GPU scheduling needs accurate resource discovery, accounting and placement. Misreported resources can cause oversubscription or idle hardware.","CONCEPTS\n\nGRES, GPU types, constraints, partitions, reservations, fair-share, job arrays and dependencies.","AI\n\nA GPU cluster is an expensive shared resource. Scheduler policy directly affects utilization and researcher experience.","LAB\n\nDesign partitions for interactive, training and long-running jobs. Add GPU constraints and a fair-share policy conceptually.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow would you stop one user from monopolizing all GPUs? Combine quotas, fair-share, partition limits and policy.","GOAL\n\nTreat scheduler policy as infrastructure economics."),
Day(212,"Slurm and storage","Avoiding I/O storms","Scheduling jobs without considering storage can create synchronized data access spikes.","PROBLEM\n\n1,000 jobs start at 09:00 and all read the same dataset. The storage platform may be healthy under normal load but collapse under synchronized startup.","SOLUTIONS\n\nStagger starts, pre-stage data, use local cache, quotas, job constraints or data-aware scheduling where appropriate.","LAB\n\nDesign a job-start policy for 256 GPUs reading 100 TB. Estimate how staggering changes instantaneous load.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy should storage engineers care about the scheduler? Because scheduler behavior shapes storage workload concurrency.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand workload orchestration as a storage input."),
Day(213,"Job arrays and workflow dependencies","Scaling many jobs safely","Job arrays and dependencies let Slurm express large workloads without manually submitting hundreds of commands.","MODEL\n\nPreprocess array → validation → training jobs → evaluation → checkpoint/archive. Dependencies enforce order.","STORAGE\n\nAvoid launching thousands of simultaneous metadata-heavy jobs. Use controlled concurrency and staging.","LAB\n\nDesign a 1,000-sample preprocessing pipeline with bounded concurrency and downstream training dependencies.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow would you prevent a job array from becoming a metadata denial-of-service against the filesystem? Discuss concurrency limits and dataset layout.","GOAL\n\nScale workflow execution responsibly."),
Day(214,"Kubernetes versus Slurm","Two orchestration worlds","Slurm dominates many traditional HPC environments; Kubernetes dominates cloud-native application platforms. AI infrastructure increasingly uses both.","COMPARE\n\nSlurm: batch/HPC resource scheduling, tightly controlled nodes, job-centric model. Kubernetes: declarative services, containers, operators, cloud-native control plane.","HYBRID\n\nA cluster may use Slurm for training and Kubernetes for services, notebooks, model serving or platform components.","LAB\n\nMap five workloads to Slurm or Kubernetes and justify each choice.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy not replace Slurm with Kubernetes everywhere? Answer from workload semantics, ecosystem maturity, scheduling behavior and operational requirements.","GOAL\n\nBecome orchestration-neutral."),
Day(215,"Run:ai and GPU orchestration concepts","GPU utilization as a scheduling problem","Modern AI platforms may use specialized GPU orchestration to improve allocation, queueing and utilization. Understand the problem before the product.","PROBLEM\n\nGPUs can be idle because of fragmentation, waiting jobs, data pipeline delays or poor placement.","SOLUTION SPACE\n\nDynamic allocation, quotas, fair-share, gang scheduling, fractional/partitioned GPU models where supported, and workload-aware placement.","STORAGE\n\nBetter GPU scheduling can increase simultaneous data demand. Storage must be sized for the resulting concurrency.","LAB\n\nDesign policy for 100 GPUs shared by research, inference and training teams.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can improving GPU scheduling expose a storage bottleneck? Because more GPUs become active concurrently.","GOAL\n\nSee compute scheduling and storage capacity as coupled."),
Day(216,"Cluster provisioning","From bare metal to ready compute node","Large AI clusters need repeatable provisioning: firmware, OS, drivers, network configuration, storage clients, monitoring and scheduler enrollment.","PIPELINE\n\nBare metal → PXE/image → OS baseline → firmware/drivers → network → storage client → monitoring → scheduler → validation.","AUTOMATION\n\nUse infrastructure-as-code/configuration management and health gates. A node should not enter production until tests pass.","LAB\n\nWrite a 20-step node onboarding checklist and identify which steps can be automated.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow do you safely add 100 nodes? Canary 1–2 nodes, validate, then roll out in batches with automatic health checks.","GOAL\n\nThink fleet-scale, not server-by-server."),
Day(217,"Cluster observability","Control-plane, node and workload telemetry","A cluster requires layered observability: infrastructure health, scheduler state, storage, network, GPU and application timing.","DASHBOARD\n\nNode health → GPU utilization → network → storage → scheduler queue → job state → application throughput.","CORRELATION\n\nA job pending may be scheduler policy; a running job with low GPU use may be storage/network/application; a node down may be hardware/OS.","LAB\n\nDesign an executive dashboard and an engineer dashboard. Explain why they show different levels of detail.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat should trigger an alert versus a dashboard observation? Alerts should represent actionable conditions, not every metric deviation.","GOAL\n\nTurn cluster telemetry into operational decisions."),
Day(218,"Cluster maintenance","Draining nodes without chaos","Maintenance requires scheduling, draining, workload awareness and rollback.","FLOW\n\nAnnounce → drain/cordon → verify jobs finished/migrated → change → health test → return to service → monitor.","STORAGE\n\nA node may be a filesystem client, storage server or both. Maintenance must respect data-path redundancy and recovery state.","LAB\n\nCreate a maintenance runbook for upgrading NIC firmware on 32 GPU nodes in batches.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is draining safer than rebooting active compute nodes? It preserves workload integrity and provides a controlled state transition.","GOAL\n\nOperate clusters with change discipline."),
Day(219,"Orchestration checkpoint","Whiteboard a production AI cluster","Combine Slurm/Kubernetes, storage and fabric into one architecture.","WHITEBOARD\n\nUsers → identity → scheduler/orchestrator → GPU nodes → storage fabric → parallel filesystem/object → monitoring/control plane.","QUESTIONS\n\nHow are GPUs allocated? How is storage mounted? How do jobs fail? How do nodes drain? How is a dataset staged? What happens during a storage outage?",
"LAB\n\nDesign a 128-GPU cluster with Slurm training, Kubernetes services, shared storage and object archive. Include management and failure domains.","INTERVIEW\n\nExplain the architecture to a storage engineer, network engineer and application owner using the terminology each needs.","STANDARD\n\nYou can now discuss AI infrastructure as an integrated platform rather than isolated technologies.")
)
